<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abencerrajes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Banu Sarraj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abencerrajes (Banu Sarraj) were a noble family of the Nasrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> who were influential at court in the fifteenth century. They acted as kingmakers in the civil wars among Nasrid claimants, notably supporting Muhammad IX, and their rivalries with other clans weakened the emirate. Legend holds that many of them were killed in a hall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> that bears their name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abencerrajes.jpg" alt="Abencerrajes" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ignacio Merino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_%C3%BAltimo_abencerraje.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from the Arabic Ibn Sarrāj, saddler's son, and appears to derive from Yussuf ben-Serragh, head of the tribe in the time of Muhammed VII, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, whom he served well in his struggles to keep the crown. Little is known of the family with certainty. The Chambers Biographical Dictionary records that they arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 8th century, but the name is familiar above all from literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The best-known telling is the romance Guerras civiles de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> by Ginés Pérez de Hita, which celebrates the feuds between the Abencerrages and the rival family of the Benedin, in Arabic banu Edin, and the cruelty the Abencerrages suffered. In the story one of the Abencerrages fell in love with a lady of the royal family and was caught climbing up to her window. The killings were ordered by Ibrahim Benedin, who had a feud with the family: the whole family was shut up in one of the halls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> and put to death. That court, one of the most beautiful of the palace, is still called the Hall of the Abencerrages.</p><p>Washington Irving, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tales-of-the-alhambra">Tales of the Alhambra</a> (1832), held the massacre to be a fiction, though he wrote that a number of Abencerrages were killed in one of the battles of the time. The legend lived on regardless. J. P. de Florian's Gonsalve de Cordoue and Chateaubriand's Le dernier des Abencerrages are adaptations of Pérez de Hita's story, and the legend appears in poems and plays such as the Romance de la pérdida de Alhama and the novella The Abencerraje, and in two operas, Luigi Cherubini's Les Abencérages and Giacomo Meyerbeer's L'esule di Granata.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abencerrages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great families such as the <b>Abencerrajes</b> (Banu Sarraj) gained strong influence at court in the fifteenth century, and their rivalries with other clans and with the ruling house fuelled the civil wars that weakened the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
